{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbwLx123XhakAaeoFpJR", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Lists** and **tuples** are both ordered sequences in Python, but they differ in these ways:\n\n| | List | Tuple |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` |\n| Mutability | Mutable (can add, remove, or change items) | Immutable (fixed after creation) |\n| Hashable | No, so it can't be a dict key or set member | Yes, if all its elements are hashable |\n| Methods | Many (`append`, `remove`, `sort`, \u2026) | Only `count` and `index` |\n| Performance | Slightly more memory, slightly slower | Smaller and a bit faster |\n\n**When to use which:**\n- **List**: for collections that change, or homogeneous data, such as a list of user names.\n- **Tuple**: for fixed records, where position carries meaning, such as `(x, y)` coordinates or `(name, age)`. Also use one for dict keys, or to signal \"this shouldn't change.\"\n\n**Gotchas:**\n- A one-element tuple needs a trailing comma: `(5,)`. Without it, `(5)` is just the integer 5.\n- Immutability is shallow. A tuple holding a list can still have that list modified: `t = ([1], 2); t[0].append(3)` works.\n\n```python\nlst = [1, 2, 3]\nlst[0] = 99        # OK\n\ntup = (1, 2, 3)\ntup[0] = 99        # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7544, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16830, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7544}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "8cdb872e-32df-4798-9b68-08ddb22b7648", "uuid": "65131f38-f4f8-41e0-b33d-7e6b1db1758f", "timestamp": "2026-10-